{"logits": "Logits are the raw, unnormalized scores output by a model before applying a softmax or sigmoid function. In the context of a sigmoid classifier, logits represent the model's confidence scores for each class before normalization."},
{"logits__calculated_via__sigmoid_classifier": "The phrase \"calculated via\" indicates the method or process used to derive the logits. In this case, the logits are derived using a sigmoid classifier, which applies a sigmoid function to the model's outputs to produce probabilities."},
{"sigmoid_classifier": "A sigmoid classifier is a type of binary classifier that uses the sigmoid function to convert the model's raw outputs (logits) into probabilities. These probabilities indicate the likelihood of the input belonging to a particular class, typically ranging between 0 and 1.|Here is another description: The sigmoid classifier is a type of binary classifier that uses a sigmoid function to transform a real-valued input into a probability value between 0 and 1. It is commonly used in machine learning for binary classification tasks."},
{"sigmoid_classifier__considers__first_dimensions_batch_size": "The predicate \"considers\" in this context refers to the sigmoid classifier taking into account or utilizing the first dimensions of the input data, specifically the batch size, as part of its calculation process for determining the logits."},
{"first_dimensions_batch_size": "The first dimensions, or batch size, refer to the number of samples processed together in a single forward/backward pass through the neural network. In this context, the sigmoid classifier uses the batch size as one of the factors in calculating the logits for each training sample."},
{"sample": "The sample refers to an individual data point or instance within a dataset that is being evaluated by a sigmoid classifier. This sample is part of a batch and is assessed based on its logit value.|Here is another description: The sample refers to an individual data point or instance within a dataset that is being evaluated for classification purposes. In the context of machine learning, particularly in fraud detection, each sample undergoes processing to determine its classification."},
{"sample__classified_as__1_fraud_if_logits_0_5": "The predicate \"classified as\" indicates the process by which the sample is assigned a category based on the output of the sigmoid classifier. This classification is determined by comparing the logit value of the sample to a threshold."},
{"1_fraud_if_logits_0_5": "The object \"1 (fraud) if logits >= 0.5\" describes the classification outcome for the sample. If the logit value of the sample is greater than or equal to 0.5, it is categorized as 1, which signifies fraud. This threshold-based classification helps in distinguishing between fraudulent and non-fraudulent samples."},
{"sample__classified_as__0_non_fraud_if_logits_0_5": "The predicate \"classified as\" indicates the action of assigning a category or label to the sample based on the outcome of a classification algorithm. In this scenario, the classification is determined by comparing the logits value to a threshold."},
{"0_non_fraud_if_logits_0_5": "The object \"0 (non-fraud) if logits < 0.5\" describes the classification result for samples whose logits value is below 0.5. These samples are deemed non-fraudulent, as indicated by the classification label \"0\". This threshold-based classification helps in distinguishing between fraudulent and non-fraudulent instances within the dataset."},
{"p": "p represents a probability value derived from a sigmoid function applied to a certain input f. It is used in the context of classifying samples as either fraud or non-fraud based on a threshold value of 0.5."},
{"p__defined_as__sigmoid_f": "The predicate \"defined as\" indicates that the subject p is explicitly described or characterized by the mathematical expression that follows, which in this case is the sigmoid function applied to f."},
{"sigmoid_f": "sigmoid(f) is a mathematical function that maps any real-valued number into a value between 0 and 1. In this context, it is used to transform the input f into a probability-like value p, which is then used for classification purposes."},
